  "narrative": "The nine differentially-abundant metabolites resolve to a single, coherent signal in the xenobiotic-metabolism network. RaMP enrichment returned five candidate pathways, all sourced from Reactome and all with FDR ≤ 2 × 10⁻⁶ (see Table). The top two by fold-enrichment are **Phase II – Conjugation of compounds** (fold 159, FDR 0.0) and **Xenobiotics** (fold 346, FDR 2 × 10⁻⁶), both driven by the same cluster of electrophilic substrates that the treatment group has either accumulated or prematurely cleared.\n\n**Key driver compounds.** Benzo[a]pyrene-7,8-dihydrodiol-9,10-oxide (C14853) is the ultimate carcinogenic epoxide downstream of benzo[a]pyrene bioactivation and is directly annotated to Phase II – Conjugation (Reactome R-HSA-156580) and its sub-pathway Glutathione conjugation (R-HSA-156590). L-Cysteine (C00097) is similarly annotated and, as the rate-limiting precursor of glutathione, anchors the conjugating capacity of the pathway. Coumarin (C05851) is annotated to both Phase I – Functionalization (R-HSA-211945) and Cytochrome P450 – arranged by substrate type (R-HSA-211897), placing it upstream of the conjugative phase. N-hydroxy-4-aminobiphenyl (C20319) and methimazole (C07190) also belong to Phase I/II functionalisation pathways and likely reflect exposure to aromatic amine xenobiotics and thionamide drugs, respectively.\n\n**Upstream/downstream relationships.** L-Cysteine and its immediate downstream product glutathione (KEGG C00051) are confirmed reachable in the KEGG reaction graph (path length 2 hops), supporting the claim that cysteine availability constrains Phase II conjugating capacity. No direct KEGG path connects L-cysteine to the benzo[a]pyrene epoxide within six hops, indicating these metabolites are likely co-exposed rather than sequentially derived.\n\n**Biological significance.** The convergence of Phase I and Phase II metabolites suggests that the treatment perturbs xenobiotic metabolism at two levels: cytochrome P450–mediated oxidation (Phase I) and glutathione-dependent conjugation (Phase II). Elevated benzo[a]pyrene epoxide paired with altered L-cysteine and coumarin levels is consistent with either increased bioactivation of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, impaired Phase II detoxification, or a combination of both. The presence of methimazole and bromide (the latter also in the Biological oxidations pathway) further points to halogenated compound exposure or redox-active drug metabolism. Together, these changes describe a multi-pathway disruption of cellular defence against electrophilic toxicants.",
